  "landfallEstimate": "Movement towards Andhra Pradesh & Yanam coasts",
  "rawBulletin": "REGIONAL SPECIALISED METEOROLOGICAL CENTRE -TROPICAL CYCLONES,\nNEW DELHI TROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO 03\nDEMS-RSMC TROPICAL CYCLONES NEW DELHI DATED 16.11.2026\nFROM: RSMC \u2013TROPICAL CYCLONES, NEW DELHI\nTO: STORM WARNING CENTRE, NAYPYITAW (MYANMAR)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, BANGKOK (THAILAND)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, COLOMBO (SRILANKA)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, DHAKA (BANGLADESH)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, KARACHI (PAKISTAN)\nMETEOROLOGICAL OFFICE, MALE (MALDIVES)\nOMAN METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT,\nMUSCAT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nYEMEN METEOROLOGICAL SERVICES,\nREPUBLIC OF YEMEN (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nNATIONAL CENTRE FOR METEOROLOGY, UAE (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nPRESIDENCY OF METEOROLOGY AND ENVIRONMENT,\nSAUDI ARABIA (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nIRAN METEOROLOGICAL ORGANISATION, (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nQATAR METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nTROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO. 03 FOR THE NORTH INDIAN OCEAN\n(THE BAY OF BENGAL AND THE ARABIAN SEA) VALID FOR THE NEXT 168\nHOURS ISSUED AT 2100 UTC OF 16.11.2026 BASED ON 1800 UTC OF\n16.11.2026.\nSub: Cyclonic Storm Akvan (pronounced a Akvan) over central parts of South\nand adjoining Westcentral Bay of Bengal\nThe cyclonic storm \u201cAkvan\u201d [Pronunciation :Akvan]\u201d over central parts of South and\nadjoining Westcentral Bay of Bengal remained practically stationary during past 06 hours\nand lay centered at 1800 UTC of today, the 16th November, 2026 over the same region,\nnear Latitude 12.2\u00b0N and Longitude 85.7\u00b0E, about 610 km east of Chennai (43279,Tamil\nNadu), 630 km northeast of Trincomalee (43418,Sri Lanka), 660 km southeast of\nMachilipatnam (43185, Andhra Pradesh), 670 km south-southeast of Visakhapatnam\n(43149, Andhra Pradesh) and 790 km south of Gopalpur (43049,Odisha).\nIt is very likely to move north-northwestwards and intensify further into a Severe Cyclonic\nStorm over Westcentral Bay of Bengal around 1200 UTC of 17th November,\n2026.Thereafter, it is likely to move northwestwards and intensify further into a very severe\ncyclonic storm around 0000 UTC of 19th November.\nContinuing to move northwestward, it is likely to cross Andhra Pradesh & Yanam coasts\nbetween Nellore (43245) and Tuni (43147), around 0600 UTC of 20th November, 2026 as a\nvery severe cyclonic storm with the maximum sustained wind speed of 120-130 kmph\ngusting to 145 kmph.\nThis is a guidance Bulletin for WMO/ESCAP Panel Member countries. Visit respective National websites\nfor Country specific Bulletins.\nThe forecast location and intensity of the system is given below:\nDate/Time Position Maximum sustained surface Category of cyclonic\n(UTC) (Lat. 0N/ long. 0E) wind speed (Kmph) disturbance\n16.11.26/1800 12.2/85.7 65-75 gusting to 85 Cyclonic Storm\n17.11.26/0000 12.4/85.6 70-80 gusting to 90 Cyclonic Storm\n17.11.26/0600 12.6/85.4 80-90 gusting to 100 Cyclonic Storm\n17.11.26/1200 12.8/85.2 90-100 gusting to 110 Cyclonic Storm\n17.11.26/1800 13.0/85.0 95-105 gusting to 115 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n18.11.26/0600 13.4/84.6 100-110 gusting to 120 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n18.11.26/1800 14.0/84.0 105-115 gusting to 125 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n19.11.26/0600 14.7/83.0 115-125 gusting to 140 Very Severe Cyclonic Storm\n19.11.26/1800 15.2/82.0 120-130 gusting to 145 Very Severe Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/0600 15.7/81.0 120-130 gusting to 145 Very Severe Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/1800 16.2/80.2 95-105 gusting to 115 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n21.11.26/0600 16.8/79.8 65-75 gusting to 85 Cyclonic Storm\n21.11.26/1800 17.4/79.5 40-50 gusting to 60 Depression\nAs per INSAT 3DS, vortex lay over the Southeast & Adjoining Southwest Bay of Bengal\nand its neighbourhood centered near 12.3N/85.7 E. Intensity of the system is characterized\nas T2.5. Associated scattered to broken low and medium-level clouds, with embedded\nintense to very intense convection lay over west central and southwest Bay (minimum\ncloud-top temperature (CTT) ranges from -70\u00b0C to -90\u00b0C) and moderate to intense\nconvection over southeast Bay, Andaman Sea and Islands (minimum cloud-top\ntemperature (CTT) ranges from -40\u00b0C to -70\u00b0C)\nThe est",
